 <p><strong>Nackgammon.</strong> Nackgammon is backgammon with a different starting position. Each player moves one checker from the 13-point and one from the 6-point to their opponent&#x27;s side, so each side starts with four back checkers instead of two. We use it to test whether a model still plays well when the opening positions are new.</p></section><section class="card about-text" id="how-it-works"><h2>How the benchmark works</h2><ul><li><strong>Leagues of full games.</strong> Each model setup plays full games against every other setup in its league. There are three leagues, and they are never merged.</li><li><strong>One decision, one request.</strong> Every move is sent as its own request. A model sees the match score, but not the moves of earlier turns or earlier games.</li><li><strong>What the model sees.</strong> Each request shows the score, the board with pip counts, the race, the dice and every legal play. Next to each play the program lists: checkers hit, points made, blots left and how many of the opponent&#x27;s 36 rolls hit them, points held, longest prime and pip counts.</li><li><strong>Two kinds of model.</strong> Decision models return a probability for every legal play. Chat models choose one play.</li><li><strong>A program judges, not a model.</strong> GNU Backgammon 1.08 scores every legal play in every position at 2-ply. No language model judges any move.</li><li><strong>Settings.</strong> Each setup is one model at one reasoning setting (off, low, medium, high). We record the time and the tokens of every decision, and its cost where a price is known.</li><li><strong>Reference programs.</strong> Three programs are shown as yardsticks and are never ranked: GNUbg weak (a weak setting of the engine, played in every league), Safest and Random. In League 1, Safest picks the play that leaves the fewest hitting rolls, then the fewest blots, then the biggest pip lead. In Leagues 2 and 3, Safest and Random are computed, not played: Safest is the engine&#x27;s best of the safest plays, Random the average of all legal plays.</li><li><strong>Late entries.</strong> A setup added after a league finished plays that league&#x27;s original field. It is ranked on the risk leaderboard, but it does not change league match-points standings, Cups, or other setups&#x27; results.</li><li><strong>Everything is public.</strong> Every position, every legal play, the engine&#x27;s value for each play and every model&#x27;s choice.</li></ul></section><section class="card about-text" id="scores"><h2>How the scores work</h2><ul><li><strong>Risk call.</strong> A position is a risk call when its legal plays leave different numbers of hitting rolls. The engine compares its best play with the best safe play: if the best play is at least 0.001 better, <strong>risk pays</strong>; otherwise <strong>safety pays</strong>.</li><li><strong>Risks found.</strong> The share of risk-pays positions where the model chose a worthwhile risky play: one at least 0.001 better than the best safe play. A risky play that is not that good counts as a wrong risk, not a risk found.</li><li><strong>Safe right.</strong> The share of safety-pays positions where the model played safe.</li><li><strong>Risk judgement.</strong> The average of risks found and safe right, from 0 to 100. A model that always plays safe scores 50. A model that gets every call right scores 100.</li><li><strong>Error rate.</strong> How much a model&#x27;s moves give away, as judged by the engine: the PR scale that human players use. Lower is better. A move that matches the engine&#x27;s best play costs nothing.</li><li><strong>Uncertainty.</strong> Each score has a 95% interval. Intervals resample whole games, not single moves, because moves inside one game are not independent. Ranks show observed scores; small gaps need not be meaningful.</li></ul><div class="how-method" id="how-method"><h3>As the Method page states it</h3><ul><li><strong>Who scores.</strong> GNU Backgammon 1.08 evaluates every legal play at 2-ply, in cubeless money equity (expected points per game, match score ignored). The loss of a decision is the equity of the best play minus the equity of the play made. <a class="small" href="../method/#error-rate">Method: Error rate</a></li><li><strong>Error rate.</strong> 500 × mean 2-ply equity loss per decision. Lower is better. This is the checker-play performance rating (PR) that backgammon players use. <a class="small" href="../method/#error-rate">Method: Error rate</a></li><li><strong>Risk judgement.</strong> The average of risks found and right safety, × 100. It does not depend on how many positions of each class a setup met. With both classes present, the Safest program scores exactly 50, the &quot;always safe&quot; line. <a class="small" href="../method/#risk-metrics">Method: Was it the right risk?</a></li><li><strong>Intervals.</strong> Intervals are 0.95 bootstrap intervals with 2000 resamples and a fixed seed (20261021). League decisions resample whole games, because moves within a game are not independent. <a class="small" href="../method/#statistics">Method: Statistics and verdicts</a></li><li><strong>Different positions.</strong> Setups played different positions; league comparisons are not paired, and risk judgement is not adjusted for how hard the positions were. <a class="small" href="../method/#limits">Method: Limits</a></li></ul><p class="table-note">Quoted from the <a href="../method/">Method</a> page.</p></div></section><section class="card about-text" id="leaderboard"><h2>Leaderboard rules</h2><ul><li><strong>One row per model.</strong> The row uses a setting fixed by rule, not the best score: the model&#x27;s highest reasoning setting. A model with one setup uses that setup.</li><li><strong>Versions.</strong> Different version names (Jev 1.13.0 and 1.14.0) are separate rows. A dated checkpoint of the same model (DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017) is the same model, and its row shows the original.</li><li><strong>Late entries count on the leaderboard,</strong> with a &quot;late entry&quot; tag, because they play the same league field.</li><li><strong>Order.</strong> Rows are sorted by risk judgement. The rank is a sort order, not a verdict. Ranks show observed scores; small gaps need not be meaningful.</li><li><strong>Tap a row</strong> to see both halves of its score, the raw counts, its error rate, its record and a link to every decision it made.</li></ul><h3 id="leaderboard-notes">How the leaderboard is built</h3>
 <p><strong>A fixed table:</strong> the filters on the Leagues page do not change it. Tap a model (or point at it) for its details and its decisions.</p>
-<p># is the rank among all models, in every view of the table's All · Chat models · Decision models filter; the programs are reference rows in every view.</p>
+<p># is the rank among all models, in every view of the table's All · Chat models · Decision models · Small models filter; the programs are reference rows in every view.</p>
+<p>Small models: 15B parameters or fewer (total), as disclosed by the maker; models with undisclosed size are not included. Parameters (in a row's details): the count its maker documents (model card, Hugging Face repository or announcement), linked; a mixture of experts as total / active; "not disclosed by maker" where the maker gives none.</p>
 <p>Bars run to each score; the whisker is its 95% interval. Ranks show observed scores; small gaps need not be meaningful. Leagues played different positions.</p>
 <p>Row rule: each model&#x27;s highest reasoning setting (off &lt; minimal &lt; low &lt; medium &lt; high &lt; xhigh &lt; max), late entries included; a model with one setup uses it; several setups at that setting: the first in league order, then by setup name, never by score.</p>
 <p>Each model's row is its highest reasoning setting played, by the effort the requests sent (off &lt; minimal &lt; low &lt; medium &lt; high &lt; xhigh &lt; max; on above off for a model without levels; a decision model's single setup), never its best score; late-entry setups count as the model's setups (a late entry stands in the league order of the league whose field it played). Several setups at that setting: the first in league order, then by setup name (no score). Every value of a row and of its details is that setup's own; its other settings are under the row's expander and under All setups. Version names are separate models; DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 is under DeepSeek V4.1 Flash (a dated checkpoint under its original). Every column of every setup: <a href="../leagues/#board-panel">Every number</a>.</p>
